course: dictionary
lesson: 6780
title: Small word, big job: or
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The word or is a joining word, called a conjunction. It joins two things and shows you can pick one of them. «Would you like tea or juice?» You can have one drink, not both, so or means choose one of these.
explain: Or can also mean if not, or otherwise. «Hurry, or you'll be late.» The clue is the warning: if you do not hurry, you will be late. You can test it by putting if not into the sentence, and it still makes sense.
explain: Or can also give another name for the same thing. «A joey, or baby kangaroo» tells you that a joey is a baby kangaroo. The word after or explains the word before it. Look for the commas, because they often mark this kind of or.
explain: Watch for set phrases like «more or less» and «or so», which mean roughly. Casual talk uses «you coming or what?», and it is a question, not a choice. When you meet or, ask yourself: is it choosing, warning, or explaining?
fact: Or is a conjunction: it joins two words, phrases or sentences.
fact: «Or» means choose one of these: «tea or juice?»
fact: «Or» can mean if not, or otherwise: «Wear a hat or you'll get burnt.»
fact: «Or» can give another name for the same thing: «a joey, or baby kangaroo.»
fact: «Either … or» gives exactly two choices, and «whether or not» means in either case.
exercise: «Do we add or subtract?» What does or do here? | It means a baby animal | It means very | *It gives a choice between two things | It joins two numbers to make a total
exercise: «Hurry, or you'll be late.» What does or mean here? | Hurry, and you will be late too | *If you do not hurry, you will be late | Hurry, because you are already late | Hurry up, you are very fast
exercise: In «a joey, or baby kangaroo», what does or show? | The joey is a different animal from the baby kangaroo | The baby kangaroo is bigger than the joey | *The joey and the baby kangaroo are the same thing | It is a choice between two animals
test: «Would you like a biscuit or an apple?» What does or show? | A warning about getting hungry | A rough guess about the time | *A choice between two things | Another name for the same thing
test: «Wear a hat or you'll get burnt.» What does or mean here? | Hats are very expensive | Burnt means the hat is hot | *If you do not wear a hat, you will get burnt | Choose between a hat and a cap
exam: In «Either the bus or the train will do», what does either … or show? | A warning that something will go wrong | Three or more choices, all equal | *Exactly two choices | Another name for the same thing
